{{nihongo|'''Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi'''|白河天皇|Shirakawa Tennō|extra=November 26, 1556 – June 6, 1625}}, also known as {{nihongo|'''Honda Heihachirō Shigetada'''|本多平八郎重忠}},  {{nihongo|'''Fumihito'''|史仁}}, and his Buddhist name {{nihongo|'''Seiganji Denzen Chushochō Yoshinobu Daikoji'''|史西岸寺殿前中書長誉良信大居士}} was the 105th Emperor of [[Okaiken|Mitoyagi]], according to the traditional order of succession. He was also the first [[Imperial bakufu]] [[shogun|shōgun]] from 1601 to 1610 and he was only person in the line to hold both the position of the emperor and shogun at the same time. 

Known as '''Fumihito''' at birth, he was abducted from the Okami Palace on March 5, 1576 by members of the Ogasawara clan in an attempt to take control of the Imperial Court as well as to persuade the Imperial Family to install [[Ogasawara Terumune]] as the shogun. The abduction is now known as the [[Yuzawa Incident]]. The Imperial Family agreed to let the Ogasawara clan take the young prince but was later abandoned by the clan at a temple in Mino. When he came of age, the Honda clan would train him as an officer within the clan and assumed the name of '''Ogasawara Taisei'''. When [[Ogasawara Terumasa]] succeeded Terumune as the daimyō of the Ogasawara clan, Shigetada became his vassal. 

He would later return to serve the Honda clan, after the Ogasawara clan offered him as a candidate to the Honda clan as the successor to the childless Shigetatsu to solidify their diplomatic partnership. Shigetada was one of the Four Honda Heavenly Pillars along with Ii Naomasa, Sakakibara Yasumasa and [[Anjō Motonobu]]. It was widely acknowledged that he was a reputed samurai and a loyal retainer of Ogasawara Terumasa. Shigetada is often referred to as "The Warrior who surpassed Death itself" because he never once suffered a significant wound, despite being the veteran of over 40 battles by the end of his life, and because he was never defeated by another samurai during his military service. He eventually became the successor of the Honda clan after being adopted by Shigetatsu who was not able to produce an heir before his death. After the death of Ogasawara Terumasa, Shigetada continued his conquest of Mitoyagi, first trying to avenge the assassination of his lord Terumasa by Takigawa Kazumasu, then by avenging the death of Anjō Motonobu by usurping the island of Yagikoshu and eventually subjugating the lands that were under the Takigawa after Kazumasu's assassination in 1598. 

In 1601, he was expected to be declared shōgun by the Imperial Family in Okami but was instead declared as the Crown Prince in addition to the shōgun title by the Emperor Komei and was forced to relinquish most of his power to his second son, [[Honda Shigetomo]] when the Emperor Komei died in 1610. His eldest son, Honda Shigemasa, was declared as the Prince Takahito and became his Imperial successor. 

As the emperor, Shirakawa oversaw the consolidation of power within the Empire and established the Honda clan as the legitimate ruler of the country. He then split his family into two distinct branches with Honda Shigetomo getting full control over the land distribution and the military while the Imperial family was further reduced into a ceremonial role. He also presided over the implementation of religious freedom with the exception of Christianity that he vehemently repressed. He also help write the [[Tale of Shikibu]], a popular novel involving the Sengoku Period as well as the rise of the Honda Shogunate. 

He was assassinated in 1625 during the Kinzō Incident for [[Honda Shigetomo]]'s involvement in reforming the Sanese Han System, which angered many of the former shōen estates. [[Ii Naotaka]], who was the perpetrator of the attacks, was defeated and killed by the forces of the Shogunate at the Battle of Ichijoudani.

==Early life and abduction==
{{Main|Yuzawa Incident}}

The emperor was born on February 25, 1556 at imperial palace through then Crown Prince Yoshihito (Emperor Komei) and the empress Sento-in. The prince was named Fumihito, in hopes that the future prince will serve all the people of Mitoyagi. The imperial family had long been removed from the political landscape, only serving as the signatory of many ''shōen'' estates that had led to the [[Sengoku period]] of Okaiken.

[[File:Yuzawa Incident.jpg|thumb|left|280px|A depiction of the Yuzawa Incident, 1558.]]
In October 12, 1558, while the Imperial Family was staying on the summer palace of Mikasawa, in the territory of the Ogasawara clan of Mino, [[Yuzawa Incident|assailants breached the walls]] of the poorly guarded palace and succeeded in kidnapping the young prince. Prince Mutsuhito, the uncle of the prince, was inadvertently assassinated during the kidnapping; The Crown Prince Yoshihito was uninjured but laments the kidnapping of his son and the death of Mutsuhito. Fumihito was taken to the residence of the Ogasawara clan in an attempt to extort the Imperial Family for more control of territory in Mino and also attempt to rule Mitoyagi through increasing their ranks. The emperor first refused the demands of the Ogasawara clan, but later backtracked after Ogasawara Terunobu threatened to kill the young prince regardless of his imperial status.  

In the process, members of the Ogasawara clan received the northern quarter of the island of Harujima and a considerable sum of 300,000 ''koku'' for the main Ogasawara clan retainers. This accounted to 1.5 million ''koku'' overall. The imperial court also appointed Terunobu a ''Bugyō'' of the imperial courts, giving him considerable power and authority of the Mitoyagi islands. However, the Ogasawara clan never exchanged Fumihito; instead, the clan claimed that he had died of disease during his journey back to the capital. The crown prince later asked the warring clans to take over the capital in order to free the imperial government from the influence of the Ogasawara. 

Fumihito was placed in the Shōhei Temple under the guidance of Zen no Kishō and was trained to become a page. He became known as "Ichimatsu" during his stay at Shōhei Temple. The Ogasawara clan would later abandon the hostaged prince at the temple due to the increasing intensity of hostilities in Mitoyagi and began their brutal unification process of the rest of the Sanese Islands in order to stop the emperor's demands to the other warring clans to take the capital from the Ogasawara clan. 

Zen no Kishō would convert Ichimatsu into Jodō-shu, or Pure Land Buddhism, in order to influence the imperial court through religion to recognize and allow the practice of Jodō-shu and Jodō-shinshu in Okaiken, which was illegal at the time. In 1561, Ogasawara Terumune was killed battling the Takehara clan in the Battle of Adachi, and was succeeded by Ogasawara Terumasa. Terumasa was known as the "Fool of Mino" and was not interested in the affairs of his father against the Imperial Family, so he burned any records of any interaction of the Ogasawara and the Imperial Family including records about the young prince.

==Forgotten and adopted==
When he came of age on February 25, 1573 Ichimatsu, serving as a page at Shohei-ji, became the temple's housekeeper. He became in charge of the festivities during the Kanto festival and adopted practices that are still being done at the temple today. He was a devout ''Jodō-shu'', in contrast to the Imperial Family's animistic beliefs. 

In March of 1573, Ichimatsu killed two bandits that were targeting him and [[Shimotsuma Rairen]], then Shohei-ji's temple guard. Proving his rudimentary skills with the ''yari'', he was sent to serve Ogasawara Terumasa's brother, Ogasawara Teruhide, and defend the town of Suemori from the invasion of the Taketoyo clan. 

On July 22, 1573 Ichimatsu was officially adopted by Honda Shigetatsu after seeing his abilities at administering the temple. Back then, Shigetatsu was a vassal under the Ogasawara. In 1574, the Ogasawara clan launched an unprecedented assault of Kuge, a neighbouring province of Mino. Shigetatsu, which happened to be childless, decided that Ichimatsu be the next leader of the clan by adopting him directly to his tutelage. Ichimatsu was quickly trained in the military arts and was given his swords and his own ''yari''. While he was reluctant to do so at first, he slowly learned combat skills. Ichimatsu remained a devout ''Jodō-shu'' member against the wishes of his adopted father, who was of the ''Nichiren'' sect. He would later encourage his adopted father to adopt ''Jodō-shu''. Ichimatsu was known to be clumsy during weaponry practice and would always scorned by Shigetatsu. Ichimatsu was a clear speaker with a strong presence about him, and was known to run around with other youths from the area, without any regard to his own rank in society. Some of them would later play a large role in the Imperial Bakufu, such as Kuroda Fukumaru and Hori Soemon. With the introduction of firearms into Mitoyagi he became known for his fondness for ''tanegashima'' guns and his spear.

In his second coming-of-age ceremony on 12 August 1574, this time as a ''bushi'', Ichimatsu adopted the name of Honda-no-Shigetada, adopting the "Shige" character from his adoptive father and "Tada" from another one of the retainers of the Ogasawara clan, Takigawa Tadakage. By this time, the Ogasawara could no longer identify if he was the prince Fumihito and perceived that he was the biological son of Honda Shigetatsu. He received his the ceremonial sword of the Honda clan and was sent to assist Ogasawara Terumasa as the representative successor to Honda Shigetatsu. He was Terumasa's sandal-bearer until Ogasawara Terumasa was ambushed at the Battle of Iwawada, where he would valiantly defend his lord from an onslaught of attackers from the Yuki clan. He and Terumasa were able to safely retreat to the Ogasawara family compound in Nagaya. Terumasa would later bestow the name ''Taisei'' to Shigetada and allowed him to adopt the Ogasawara family name for his service to Terumasa.

==Sengoku samurai==
{{Campaigns of Honda Shigetada| embed=yes}}

Shigetada participated in his second battle in February 1575 at the Battle of Sakaide. Terumasa subsequently gave him retainer status along with Takigawa Kazumasu, Takigawa Tadakage's son. He received a stipend of 2,000 ''koku'' from Terumasa in Mino as a reward for his promotion to retainer status. However, in April of 1575, A succession crisis loomed over the Ogasawara clan. Ogasawara Teruhide, the younger brother of Terumasa, staged a revolt against him. Terumasa, collecting about a thousand men, suppressed the hostile members of his family and their allies. Ogasawara Teruhide was defeated by Terumasa at the Battle of Awa, but escaped to Suemori castle, where Terumasa had assigned Shigetada and Takigawa Kazumasu to lay siege upon. The siege was successful and Teruhide was forced to commit ''seppuku''. Terumasa rewarded Shigetada a long spear called the ''tonbōgiri'' and an additional 10,000 ''koku'' to him. 

In July of 1576, Terumasa sent Shigetada to protect Suzuki Yasutomo in the Siege of Okawanishi. Yasutomo had defected to Terumasa's side from Taketoyo Sadahiro, a daimyo from Musashino Province, one of the most powerful men in Harujima. 

By 1578, Terumasa had captured and obliterated the fortress of Iwakura, eliminated all opposition within the Ogasawara clan, and established his uncontested rule in Mino Province. Shigetada was given a stipend of another 10,000 ''koku'' (with a total of 22,000 ''koku'') and ownership of the damaged Suemori castle, which with the help of the ''hatamoto'' of the Honda clan, was rebuilt into the current Nagashino Castle in 1581. 

In July of 1579, Shigetada met his first and primary wife Hatsune-no-kata in the town of Mikata in the neighbouring province of Kōchi while laying siege to the castle town of Nakaoka. She was the daughter of the Lord of Mikata Castle, [[Anjō Motonobu]], who had earlier defected to the Ogasawara from the Taketoyo due to Sadahiro's poor treatment of his clan. To bid for his survival of the clan, Hatsune-no-kata was going to be sent to Terumasa in order for him to be taken as his wife but would decline the offer. Shigetada, for reasons unknown to this day, instead offered to be her husband. Later, the Anjō would become primary vassals of the Honda clan and would take various high level government positions in the ''bakufu''. In 4 October 1580, their first child, Nabenosuke (later the Prince Takahito, then the Emperor Daigo), was born. 

In 23 June 1581, Shigetada received the news that the head of the Honda clan and his adoptive father Honda Shigetatsu was ill and wanted Shigetada to take his place as the leader of the Honda clan. Terumasa approved of the move on 8 August 1581, and Shigetada changed his name back to Honda and adopted the name ''Heihachirō'', a name that was passed down to the first-born son or the presumed successor for many generations. In 18 November 1581 Shigetatsu would later succumb to his illness, and Shigetada became the head of the Honda clan. Ogasawara Terumasa allowed Shigetada to move the Honda clan from Nagaya to Suemori with a degree of independence not seen with the other Ogasawara retainers.

In 1582, his second son, [[Honda Shigetomo|Naiki]], was born from his primary wife; it was followed by the birth of his third son, [[Honda Shigetoki|Naimon]] from a concubine three months later. In 14 July 1582, he participated in the Battle of Uomura, where he famously defeated Yamanaka Shikanosuke in a duel. Shikanosuke would later join the Ogasawara as a retainer for Takigawa Kazumasu. After the Battle of Uomura, Shigetada would later venture and attack territories in Kaga, where he was able to defeat the small Hachisuka clan at the Seige of Wakamatsu.

==Leader of Honda clan==
[[File:Honda_Shigetada.png|200px|thumb|left|A contemporary image of Honda Shigetada, generated from the bone records and the use of Satori AI.]]
As the leader of Suemori (Nagashino) castle, Shigetada was tasked by Terumasa to take advantage of the weakening Taketoyo clan and take over Musashino province. In April of 1584 he lay siege to the Taketoyo clan's castle at Temmokuzan and was successful. Taketoyo Sadayori, the adopted son of Taketoyo Sadahiro, committed ''seppuku''. When the once powerful Taketoyo clan was defeated, Ogasawara Terumasa gained greatly in prestige, and many samurai and warlords pledged fealty to him. Subsequent victories by Takigawa Kazumasu in Kuge and Shibata Gonroku in Kōchi consolidated Terumasa's control over the northern half of the Island of Harujima. 

In 14 December 1585, Honda clan forces headed by Anjō Motonobu attacked the Yamagata clan in the battle of Yunami but was defeated by Yamagata Masakage and was forced to retreat to Nagashino. In February of 1586, another attack was launched by the Uesugi from the island of Yagikoshu in retaliation to the destruction of the Taketoyo clan in Musashino, of which they were allied with. Already defeated in Yunami and significantly weakened by disease and a rare typhoon in January of 1585, the Honda clan lost the majority of its control of Musashino and the Uesugi was on the verge of invading Mino through the breakthrough. 

===Failures, then successes in Musashino===

Terumasa would recall Shigetada from Nagashino after his failure and was asked to commit ''seppuku'' in Nagaya castle but was later encouraged by the rest of his vassals not to commit as Shigetada was facing two powerful clans at the same time without being helped by the rest of the Ogasawara allies; if Shigetada was to commit ''seppuku'', the Ogasawara would become vulnerable in Mino from the attacks by the Uesugi. Terumasa agreed to send Shigetada back into Nagashino to continue the defense of Musashino from the Yamagata and the Uesugi. Takigawa Kazumasu would provide assistance and both were able to wrest Musashino from the Yamagata by November of 1586. The Honda clan also won a decisive battle in the Battle of Yokosuka on 13 November 1586 where Shigetada and less than 500 troops were able to suppress the Uesugi's 7,000-strong army from taking over the strategic Yokosuka castle. The Uesugi would later retreat to Yagikoshu on 18 January 1587. Takigawa Kazumasu and Honda Shigetada would later restart its invasion of the Yamagata clan at the battle of Morihama where Yamagata Masakage was captured and was thought to be ordered executed. Instead, Takigawa Kazumasu would forgive him and encouraged him to join his forces in order to keep his territory in Iwami but with a reduction from 120,000 ''koku'' to 32,000 ''koku''. Honda Shigetada was given about 20,000 ''koku'' for helping Takigawa Kazumasu.

Ogasawara Terumasa would send Takigawa Kazumasu and Maeda Toshiie to the imperial court in 14 June 1587 to install Terumasa as the ''daijō-danjo''. The imperial court agreed to have both Kazumasu and Toshiie meet the emperor. To their surprise, the Emperor Komei looked similar to Honda Shigetada and had asked if he was Honda Shigetada himself. The emperor declined that he was Honda Shigetada, but mentioned about the abduction of his first born and had thought that he had been killed on the way back to the capital. The emperor believed that the now crown prince is still alive and if he was proven wrong, then the succession would continue on but become endangered with the Princess Tomoko, who was childless and would have to adopt a prince from the military families of Okaiken. Kazumasu mentioned that the Ogasawara abandoned Shigetada on a temple and it was Honda Shigetatsu that ultimately adopted him to the Honda clan. In spite of the revelations, the emperor agreed to install Terumasa as the ''daijō-danjo'' but would mention to Kazumasu and Toshiie that if Terumasa and the Ogasawara were to endanger the imperial court and Honda Shigetada again, both Kazumasu and Toshiie are granted powers to overrule Terumasa and kill him without punishment. This would later influence the betrayal of Takigawa Kazumasu against Ogaswara Terumasa in 1592.

===First invasion of Yagikoshu, 1587-1590===
[[File:本多重忠牧野.png|350px|thumb|left|A woodblock print of Honda Shigetada after his defeat in the Battle of Makino.]]
In Nagaya, Ogasawara Terumasa would task Honda Shigetada with the invasion of Yagikoshu on 19 October 1587. Shigetada would land on a village about 17 kilometres northeast of Toyozawa and establish the castle town of Sakai. Shigetada suppressed the counterattack by Yano Nobumori in the battle of Otsuka in 26-27 of November 1587 and later destroy the Yano clan in the invasion of the Kinai Peninsula on 14 February 1588. The Uesugi, who is based on Toyozawa, attacked the Southern Kinai peninsula on 19 February 1588 and defeated the forces of Honda Shigetada led by Ikeda Tsuneoki at the Battle of Kosaka. The Honda forces were pushed back to Sakai by October of 1588 but the forces led by Honda Shigetada was able to win decisively at the Battle of Higashi-Sakai on 12–18 November 1588. After the complete capitulation of the Seto Peninsula, the Honda forces split into 2 groups: The Left Army, led by Shigetada, was tasked to attack Southern Yagikoshu to relieve the pressure from the Yamauchi clan, who had allied itself with the Ogasawara. The Right Army, led by Anjō Motonobu, was tasked to invade the Uesugi Territories in Okayama by a naval invasion from their base in Sakai.

The Right Army enjoyed a string of Victories in the Battles of Hachimon and Itsukaichi in mid-1589 but were defeated in the Battle of Tsutsui on 2 September 1589. Anjō Motonobu was killed in the Battle of Mogami after a surprise attack by the Uesugi by flooding the Sai River on 30 September 1589. The Left Army was driven back into Seto by 12 January 1590, where Sakakibara Yasuhide signed a non-aggression pact with the Uesugi on behalf of Shigetada. Meanwhile, Shigetada was able to control the Seto Peninsula coast of Yagikoshu by securing victories in the Battles of Tedori, Uwakai and Kago in mid-1589. The Yamauchi were relieved from the invasion of the Satake clan by Honda Shigetada and were able to drive the Satake out of the Yamauchi clan territories in Kurosaki by 2 July 1589. However, the Honda clan's control over the Seto coast of Yagikoshu was short-lived. With the death of Anjō Motonobu at the Battle of Mogami, the Uesugi attacked the Honda clan's supply lines and Shigetada was cut off from Sakai. The Right Army was defeated at the Battle of Makino on 20 December 1589 and Shigetada was forced to retreat to Harujima on 14 March 1590. Sakai was destroyed by the Uesugi forces and moved its residents to Toyozawa.

===Punishment by Terumasa and the Incident in Okami, 1590-1592===
[[File:Honda Shigetada 2.jpg|thumb|right|240px|A depiction of Honda Shigetada in 1590.]]
Upon returning to Nagashino, Ogasawara Terumasa reprimanded Shigetada for his failure of the invasion of Yagikoshu. The Honda clan's holdings in Musashino were reduced to 10,200 ''koku'' and Shigetada's wives and children were to be sent in the newly-completed Otomo castle to be kept as hostages. Shigetada lamented about the loss of more than three quarters of territory and his children and himself had offered to commit ''seppuku'' to Terumasa for his failures during the invasion of Northern Yagikoshu. However, Ogasawara Terumasa declined, saying that Shigetada's punishment would be slow and painful for being indecisive; which led to the death of one of his retainers. Shigetada returned to Nagashino in despair and became a hermit from September of 1590 to March of 1591, writing no letters to his wives, his vassals or his children. Many people in Nagashino did not see him do political affairs and with dealing with the Ogasawara either; instead Shigetada was in the streets and shops of Nagashino to talk to its citizens, its merchants and peasants that supply Nagashino. It was believed that many of Shigetada's mercantile and agricultural laws that were implemented by the ''bakufu'' came from his interaction with local citizens. It was also here where Shigetada would meet Ii Naomasa, one of his four pillars, on a weapons shop. Naomasa had become a ''rōnin'' after the death of Taketoyo Sadahiro and his expulsion from Taketoyo Sadayori's vassals. Honda Shigetada employed Ii Naomasa in 2 January 1589 and Naomasa immediately offered to send his two children, [[Ii Naotaka|Michiyo]] and [[Suwa Hidetoshi|Takechiyo]] to Otomo Castle as hostages.

In 18 March 1591, seeing Honda Shigetada's failure in securing the northern coast of Yagikoshu, Takigawa Kazumasu was tasked with the planned second invasion of Yagikoshu with the help of Yamagata Masakage. Terumasa  targeted an invasion date of mid-March 1592. During the preparation period however, Takigawa Kazumasu saw a change in Ogasawara Terumasa's attitude that reminded him of the Ogasawara Terumune's demeanour during the Yuzawa Incident. Terumasa was more aggressive and more impatient; he would pay attention to every single detail of the invasion force, from the length of their spears to armour. Terumasa had also grown suspicious of Honda Shigetada, who had not answered the letters from him; neither did his own family and his vassals received any word from Shigetada about Terumasa's requests, even though they were in danger of being killed by the Ogasawara. In 3 February 1582, Terumasa would send an unusual expeditionary force of 800 people against Shigetada, which was unprecedented since Shigetada was his own vassal.

Terumasa would also force the Imperial Court, including Emperor Komei, to move to Otomo Castle by late May 1592, which is unprecedented during that period as the emperor is not allowed to leave the Imperial Palace complex unless under extreme distress such as a foreign invasion. Takigawa Kazumasu became extremely concerned as he knew that the repercussions of the Imperial Family being moved to Otomo Castle to be potentially slaughtered by Terumasa and Shigetomo's life being put into danger would destabilize Mitoyagi and put it very deep into civil war. Takigawa Kazumasu would send an expeditionary 1,000-strong force towards Nagashino to protect Shigetada while moving the majority of his troops towards the Port of Izu with a stopover in Okami in order to stop and assassinate Ogasawara Terumasa.

As planned, Ogasawara Terumasa would stay in Okami's Kofukuji temple. In 21 April 1592, the same day that Terumasa would perish in the Kofukuji coup, the Takigawa army led by Maeda Toshiie successfully repelled the small contingent of Ogasawara soldiers and would notify Honda Shigetada about the betrayal of Takigawa Kazumasu. The Honda clan would quickly group and proceeded to attack Otomo Castle in 3 March 1592, successfully freeing the children and wives of the Honda clan and would cause the destruction of the Otomo castle. Honda Shigetada would return to Nagashino and immediately make amends with Takigawa Kazumasu who now controls the majority of the Ogasawara clan's holdings in Harujima. Takigawa Kazumasu restored Honda Shigetada's rice allocation to 125,000 ''koku'' and allow his vassals to return service to him.

==Reunification of Mitoyagi==
[[File:Shigetada Defense of Suemori.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A woodblock print of Honda Shigetada resting after he successfully repelled the Takigawa Forces at the Defense of Suemori.]]

===Conflict with Takigawa Kazumasu===
After the events of 1592, the relationship between [[Honda Shigetada]] and [[Takigawa Kazumasu]] had become strained. Although they had made amends and restored some level of trust, tensions were still high.

In 1593, the conflict between the two escalated. Takigawa became increasingly concerned with the growing power of the Honda clan and their alliance with the powerful Sone clan. He feared that this could upset the delicate balance of power in the region and potentially lead to his own downfall. Takigawa's suspicions were confirmed when he discovered that Honda Shigetada was secretly amassing a large army. He believed that Shigetada was planning to attack and take over his territories. In response, Takigawa sent a messenger to Shigetada, demanding that he disband his army and submit to his authority.

Shigetada, however, refused to back down. He argued that his army was necessary for the defense of his lands and that he had no intention of attacking Takigawa. Despite these reassurances, Takigawa remained suspicious and decided to take preemptive action. In early summer of 1593, Takigawa led a surprise attack on Honda Shigetada's castle in [[Suemori]]. The attack caught the Honda forces off guard, and they suffered heavy losses. Shigetada himself narrowly escaped with his life.

The attack sparked a full-scale conflict between the two sides. Honda Shigetada rallied his forces and launched a counter-attack on Takigawa's territories. The fighting was fierce and lasted for several months, with both sides suffering heavy losses. In the end, neither side was able to gain a decisive victory. The conflict ended in a stalemate, with both sides agreeing to a ceasefire.

===Second invasion of Yagikoshu, 1596===
Following the stalemate at Suemori, Takigawa Kazumasu sought to direct Honda Shigetada's growing military ambition away from the central capital. In February 1596, Kazumasu officially sanctioned the Second Invasion of Yagikoshu, tasking Shigetada with the subjugation of the [[Uesugi clan]], a feat Shigetada had failed to accomplish seven years prior.

Unlike the disastrous campaign of 1589, Shigetada adopted a methodical approach. Instead of a two-pronged pincer movement, he concentrated his forces into a single, massive spearhead. He appointed [[Ii Naomasa]] as the vanguard commander, whose "Red Devil" cavalry units proved instrumental in breaking the Uesugi defensive lines at the [[Battle of Kawanakajima]]. Shigetada himself commanded the main army, utilizing the new "rolling volley" arquebus tactics developed during the stalemate with Takigawa. By October 1596, the key fortress of [[Kasugayama Castle|Kasugayama]] had fallen, and the Uesugi clan was forced to capitulate, fleeing to the northern tip of the island. This victory redeemed Shigetada's reputation and secured his status as the premier field commander of the age.

===Lord of Northern Yagikoshu===
With the Uesugi defeated, Takigawa Kazumasu appointed Shigetada as the Governor-General of Northern Yagikoshu ({{nihongo|Kita-Yagi Sōtoku|北八木総督}}). This was a political maneuver intended to keep Shigetada occupied with administration far from the center of power in Harujima.

However, Shigetada turned this exile into an opportunity. He established his base at [[Yokosuka, Miyagi|Yokosuka]], transforming the sleepy port town into a major military hub. Between 1597 and 1599, he implemented the ''Rakuichi Rakuza'' (free market) policies in the north, revitalizing the war-torn region's economy. He also seized control of the Sado gold mines, which provided the Honda clan with the immense financial capital needed to bribe wavering daimyos and purchase high-quality firearms from foreign traders. It was during this period that the "Honda Military Machine" was truly forged, independent of Takigawa oversight.

===Invasion of Ishijima===
[[File:Samurai naval landing.jpg|thumb|left|200px|Honda forces landing at Urado Bay, 1600.]]
In late 1599, seeking to secure the southern trade routes, Shigetada launched an unauthorized invasion of [[Ishijima]] (formerly known in older texts as Aidashu). The island was controlled by the [[Chosokabe clan]], fierce naval warriors who had long harassed Honda shipping.

Shigetada utilized a blockade strategy, using his superior funding to hire the [[Murakami Navy]] to cut off the Chosokabe's supplies. The campaign was swift and brutal. The Honda forces landed at [[Urado Bay]] on January 14, 1600, and engaged the Chosokabe at the [[Battle of Nonichi]]. Although the Chosokabe fought fiercely, they could not withstand the sheer numbers of the Honda ground forces. [[Chosokabe Motochika]] surrendered in March 1600, pledging his navy to the Honda cause. This victory gave Shigetada control over two of the three major islands of Mitoyagi, effectively surrounding the Takigawa power base in Harujima.

===Death of Takigawa Kazumasu===
The tension between the Takigawa and Honda clans reached its breaking point in the summer of 1600. Takigawa Kazumasu, alarmed by Shigetada's unauthorized conquest of Ishijima, declared Shigetada a traitor to the imperial court. He mustered a massive coalition army, including the [[Hōjō clan|Hōjō]], [[Mōri clan|Mōri]], and [[Shimazu clan|Shimazu]] clans, ostensibly to "punish the insolent Honda."

However, fate intervened before a formal declaration of war could be executed. During the suppression of a minor peasant rebellion in the [[Kanto region]], instigated by Hōjō remnants, Takigawa Kazumasu was shot from his horse. Contemporary accounts differ on the specifics; the ''[[Mitsukoji]]'' claims it was a stray bullet from a rogue Chosokabe mercenary, while Takigawa loyalists claimed it was an assassination by Honda ninja. Kazumasu died of his wounds on June 18, 1600.

His death shattered the central administration. His heir, [[Takigawa Kazutoyo]], was young and inexperienced, leading to the immediate dissolution of the Council of Four Elders. The daimyos of Okaiken immediately split into two factions: the Western Army, loyal to the traditional Takigawa succession, and the Eastern Army, rallying behind the military might of Honda Shigetada.

===Battle of Tone===
{{Main|Battle of Tone}}
[[File:Battle_of_Tone_Screen.jpg|thumb|right|300px|The "Screen of the Crimson Fog," a 17th-century depiction of the chaotic melee at Tone.]]
The struggle for succession culminated in the Battle of Tone ({{nihongo|Tone-gawa no Tatakai|利根川の戦い}}) on October 21, 1600. Fought on the fog-shrouded floodplains of the [[Tone River]], it remains the bloodiest single day in Sanese history, often referred to by historians as the "Field of Broken Swords."

The battle involved over 160,000 men: 82,000 under the banner of the Eastern Army (Honda Shigetada) and roughly 78,000 forming the Western Army (Takigawa Loyalists/Mashita Morihiro). What began as a strategic deployment descended into absolute chaos due to dense fog and a series of cascading betrayals.

The engagement began blindly at dawn, with vanguard units stumbling into one another in the mist. However, at 10:00 AM, a shock betrayal occurred within the Honda ranks. [[Tsutsui Sadatsugu]], commanding the Honda right flank, suddenly turned his muskets on his own allies, killing vanguard commander [[Asano Tadamoto]] and exposing Shigetada’s main camp to direct assault.

Believing the battle lost, Shigetada donned his iconic antlered helmet and personally led his ''Hatamoto'' guard into the fray to stabilize the line. It was at this critical juncture, around noon, that the second, decisive betrayal occurred. [[Koyama Hidemoto]], positioned on Mount Tenjin and ostensibly allied with the West, was provoked by a Honda volley and charged down the mountain—crashing into the Western Army's flank instead of the East.

The conflicting betrayals resulted in a catastrophic breakdown of order. The battlefield dissolved into a confused, circular melee known as the "Crimson Fog," where units fought indiscriminately in the mud. By late afternoon, the sheer weight of the Koyama intervention pinned the Western Army against the swelling Tone River, leading to their total annihilation.

The victory was pyrrhic. The Honda forces suffered nearly 50% casualties (approximately 41,000 killed or wounded), while the Western Army was effectively erased with 75% casualties (nearly 60,000 men). The sheer loss of life decimated the military caste of Mitoyagi, necessitating a generation of peace simply because there were few soldiers left to fight.

==The Restoration of Prince Fumihito==

The sequence of events leading to the public acknowledgement of [[Honda Shigetada]] as the lost Imperial Prince Fumihito is known in Sanese historiography as the Restoration of 1601 ({{nihongo|Fumihito no Fukken|史仁の復権}}). While rumors of Shigetada's lineage had circulated since the Imperial audience of 1587, the official confirmation occurred during the Victory Ceremony following the [[Battle of Tone]].

===The Audience of January 12===
On January 12, 1601, Honda Shigetada entered the temporary Imperial court at [[Okami]] to formally report his victory over the Western Army. By this time, [[Emperor Komei of Mitoyagi|Emperor Komei]] was bedridden, suffering from the advanced stages of tuberculosis. The audience was intended to be a brief, ceremonial transfer of the title of ''Sei-i Taishōgun'' to Shigetada, legitimizing his military control.

According to the Jikki, Shigetada approached the dais wearing full ceremonial armor rather than court robes. During the presentation of the "Trophies of Tone"—which included the swords of the defeated generals—Shigetada removed his gauntlets to perform the ritual purification of hands.

===The Tantō of the Fifth Month===
Contemporary accounts state that Emperor Komei became visibly agitated upon seeing a small, worn ''mamori-gatana'' (protection dagger) tucked into Shigetada's sash. The weapon was distinctly smaller and older than the samurai's standard armaments, appearing more like a talisman than a tool of war. The Emperor requested to see the blade. Shigetada hesitated, noting that it was a keepsake found with him when he was abandoned at [[Shōheiji]], but ultimately presented it to the throne.

The dagger was identified as the Tantō of the Fifth Month, a ceremonial blade commissioned by Empress Sento-in for Prince Fumihito's protection shortly before the [[Yuzawa Incident]] in 1558. The tang of the blade bore a unique inscription of the Prince's childhood name, which matched the Imperial records perfectly.

===The Sign of the Dragon===
To verify the identity beyond the possession of the dagger, the Emperor ordered Shigetada to bare his left shoulder. He was looking for a specific birthmark known only to the Imperial couple—a cluster of three moles shaped like a rising dragon ({{nihongo|Ryū no Aza|竜の痣}}).

When Shigetada revealed his shoulder, the court gasped. Not only was the birthmark present, but the skin around it was completely unmarred. Despite Shigetada being a veteran of over forty battles and the bloody melee at Tone, his body bore no scars. Emperor Komei reportedly wept, declaring that this supernatural preservation was proof of the "Divine Wind" protecting the Imperial line. He stated that only a true Son of Heaven could walk through fires of war for forty years and remain untouched.

===The Imperial Decree===
Overcome with emotion, the Emperor Komei broke protocol by descending from the dais to embrace the warlord. In the presence of the gathered daimyō—including the treacherous [[Ogasawara clan|Ogasawara]] vassals—the Emperor declared that the "General of the North" was, in fact, his "Son of the South."

The revelation transformed the political landscape instantly. Shigetada was no longer merely a usurper or a powerful warlord; he was the legitimate heir to the Chrysanthemum Throne. This dual legitimacy allowed for the unprecedented Edict of Restoration, issued the following day, which formally erased the name "Honda Heihachirō" from the court registry and reinstated him as Crown Prince Fumihito.

===Pacification of Rengoshu===
Despite the victory at Tone, pockets of resistance remained, particularly in the Rengoshu (The Union of Provinces), a coalition of warrior-monks and minor lords in the mountainous central region of Harujima who refused to recognize the new Shogun-Prince.

Shigetada spent 1602 to 1605 conducting the Rengoshu Pacification Campaigns. Rather than brute force, he employed a strategy of "Sword and Sutra." He utilized his status as a devout [[Jodō-shu]] practitioner to negotiate with the religious orders, granting them protection in exchange for disarmament. For the secular lords who refused to submit, he unleashed Ii Naomasa. The campaign ended with the burning of the [[Enryaku-ji]] fortress, signaling the end of independent religious paramilitary power in Okaiken.

===Relocation of Power from Mito to Okami===
Recognizing that the old imperial capital of [[Mito]] was too steeped in the archaic traditions of the court nobles (''kuge'') and geographically vulnerable, Shigetada ordered the relocation of the government to his military base in Okami (formerly Ichioka).

Beginning in 1603, massive construction projects transformed the fishing village into a grand metropolis. Shigetada ordered the ''Tenka-busin'' (public works), forcing the defeated daimyos to contribute stone and timber for the construction of [[Okami Castle]] (the future Imperial Palace) to deplete their treasuries. Canals were dug, and the [[Okami Harbor]] was reshaped to accommodate heavy trade ships. By 1608, Okami had surpassed Mito as the political and economic center of the nation, marking the beginning of the "Honda Period."

===Resignation as Shogun and entry into Imperial Court===
On November 8, 1610, Emperor Komei passed away. In accordance with the laws of succession, Shigetada ascended to the Chrysanthemum Throne as [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Emperor Shirakawa]].

To prevent the concentration of power from destabilizing the administration, and to ensure the survival of his military legacy, Shirakawa formally resigned his position as Shogun. He transferred the title and the day-to-day military administration to his capable second son, [[Honda Shigetomo]], while his eldest son, [[Emperor Daigo of Mitoyagi|Prince Takahito]], was designated as the Imperial heir. This separation of powers established the unique Imperial-Shogunate system, where Shigetada ruled as the absolute monarch from the palace, while Shigetomo enforced his will as the military governor, a dynamic that would define the early 17th century.

==Emperor of Mitoyagi==
===Separation of Imperial Court and the ''Bakufu''===
{{Main|Dual Sovereignty Period}}
Upon his ascension to the Chrysanthemum Throne on November 8, 1610, Shirakawa (Shigetada) faced a unique constitutional crisis. He was the first individual in Sanese history to hold both the divine lineage of the Emperor and the supreme military authority of the Shogun. To resolve this, Shirakawa enacted the '''Edict of the Two Realms''' ({{nihongo|Ryōkai-no-Rei|両界の令}}) in 1611.

This edict formally separated the functions of the state into two spheres: the ''Kuge'' (Court) and the ''Buke'' (Warrior). Shirakawa retained the title of Emperor and the ultimate sovereign authority, managing foreign diplomacy, religious rites, and the appointment of high officials. He abdicated the title of ''Sei-i Taishōgun'' to his second son, [[Honda Shigetomo]], who was tasked with the administration of the ''han'' (domains), tax collection, and infrastructure maintenance.

Unlike previous eras where the Emperor was a figurehead puppet of the Shogun, Shirakawa utilized his military background to ensure the Imperial Court remained the dominant partner. He established the ''Kin-chū Narabini Kuge Shohatto'' (Laws for the Imperial Court and Nobles), which ironically restricted the power of the traditional nobility while cementing his own absolute control. The Shogunate in [[Okami]], under Shigetomo, functioned effectively as the enforcement arm of the Imperial will, creating a powerful "Dual Sovereignty" that brought stability to the war-torn nation.

===Repression of Christianity===
[[File:Christian_Martyrs_Okaiken.jpg|thumb|left|220px|A 17th-century painting depicting the expulsion of foreign missionaries from the port of Yokosuka.]]
While Shirakawa was a devout practitioner of [[Jōdo-shū]] (Pure Land Buddhism) and generally promoted religious tolerance—famously inviting the militant warrior-monks of the Rengoshu back into society—he harbored a deep suspicion of Christianity ({{nihongo|Kirishitan|吉利支丹}}).

The [[Battle of Tone]] had decimated the country's military manpower, and Shirakawa feared that foreign powers, particularly the Insulonians and their Jesuit missionaries, would exploit this weakness to foment rebellion among the peasantry. His suspicions were confirmed in 1612 during the '''Okamoto Incident''', where a Christian daimyō was discovered smuggling gunpowder to former Takigawa loyalists in [[Yagikoshu]].

In response, Shirakawa issued the '''Expulsion Edict of 1614'''. This decree banned the practice of Christianity within Mitoyagi, ordered the destruction of churches in [[Ishijima]], and expelled all foreign missionaries. Unlike the piecemeal bans of the past, this was enforced with brutal efficiency by the Honda secret police (''Metsuke''). Converts were forced to trample on images of Christ (''fumi-e'') to prove their loyalty to the Emperor. This policy of isolationist spiritualism was intended to unify the populace under the "Divine Land" ideology, positioning the Emperor as the supreme spiritual authority over any foreign god.

===''Bakufu''-centred Worldview===
Despite his isolationist stance on religion, Shirakawa was a pragmatist regarding trade. He sought to reconstruct the Sanese economy, which had been shattered by decades of civil war. However, he refused to engage in the traditional tribute system dominated by continental empires. Instead, he constructed a "Bakufu-centred" diplomatic order ({{nihongo|Okami-shugi|大上主義}}).

Under this system, the Honda Shogunate issued "Vermilion Seal Permits" ({{nihongo|Shu-in jō|朱印状}}), authorizing specific merchant ships to trade with foreign nations under the protection of the Sanese flag. This allowed Shirakawa to monopolize foreign trade profits while strictly controlling the flow of information and goods.

He treated foreign envoys, such as the Governor-General of Malatoa, [[Ernesto Lacuña]], not as diplomatic equals, but as tribute-bearers to the Imperial Court. By funneling all foreign interaction through the port of [[Yokosuka]] and the administrative hub of Okami, Shirakawa successfully projected an image of Mitoyagi as a self-sufficient empire, independent of outside influence.

===Retirement from Office===
{{Main|Insei System (Okaiken)}}
[[File:Shirakawa aged 63.png|thumb|right|240px|A depiction of the retired emperor.]]
In 1619, at the age of 63, Shirakawa formally abdicated the throne in favor of his eldest son, [[Emperor Daigo of Mitoyagi|Prince Takahito (Emperor Daigo)]]. However, this was a strategic move rather than a withdrawal from politics. Shirakawa adopted the title of ''Daijō Tennō'' (Retired Emperor) and established a "Cloistered Rule" ({{nihongo|Insei|院政}}) from the [[Toyozawa Castle]].

Freed from the ceremonial constraints of the court and the daily bureaucracy of the Shogunate, the Retired Emperor focused entirely on the grand strategy of the realm. It was during this period (1619–1625) that he, alongside Shigetomo, drafted the controversial '''Kinzō Reforms'''. These reforms aimed to dismantle the traditional land-ownership rights of the samurai class to prevent another civil war like the [[Battle of Tone]].

Operating from Toyozawa, Shirakawa acted as the true power behind the throne, balancing the interests of the new Emperor Daigo and the Shogun Shigetomo. His retirement period was characterized by a frantic push for modernization, as if he sensed his time was running out. This aggressive centralization of power ultimately alienated the conservative ''fudai'' daimyos, setting the stage for the tragic [[Kinzō Incident]] in 1625.

==Death==
{{Main|Kinzō Incident}}
[[File:Burning_of_Toyozawa.png|thumb|left|250px|''The Night of Blue Fire'', a woodblock print depicting the burning of Toyozawa Castle.]]
By early 1625, the political atmosphere in [[Okaiken]] was volatile. The [[Kinzō Reforms]], authored by the Retired Emperor [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Shirakawa]] (Shigetada) and enforced by Shogun Shigetomo, had stripped the land-owning rights from the ''fudai'' daimyō class. While Shigetomo managed the bureaucracy in [[Okami]], the resentment focused on his father at the retirement palace in [[Toyozawa]].

On the night of June 6, 1625, a coalition of disgruntled vassals led by [[Ii Naotaka]]—the son of the Honda clan's greatest hero, [[Ii Naomasa]]—launched a surprise coup d'état. This event, known as the '''Kinzō Incident''' ({{nihongo|Kinzō no Hen|金造の変}}), resulted in the death of the Retired Emperor and the psychological collapse of the Shogun.

===Assassination of Shirakawa===
According to the ''[[Mitsukoji]]'', Shigetomo received an urgent missive at Okami Castle at midnight on June 6, stating that "The Red Devils are marching on Toyozawa." Realizing that the Ii clan's elite unit had turned traitor, Shigetomo immediately mobilized 5,000 cavalry and rode hard for Toyozawa, covering a two-day journey in eighteen hours.

He arrived too late. The Ii forces, aided by internal traitors from the [[Ogasawara clan]], had breached the outer walls of Toyozawa Castle under the cover of a thunderstorm.

Contemporary accounts describe the death of Shigetada as a "battle, not an assassination." Despite being 69 years old, the Retired Emperor donned his ceremonial armor and wielded his legendary spear, ''[[Tonbogiri]]''. Reports state that Shigetada held the bridge to the Inner Sanctum (''Honmaru'') alone for nearly an hour, allowing his female attendants and the young Prince [[Honda Shigezane]] to escape. Shigetada reportedly slew over thirty attackers before his spear shaft was shattered by musket fire.

Confronted by Ii Naotaka in the burning courtyard, Shigetada refused to commit ''seppuku''. He declared that he was the Emperor, and only Heaven could command his death. Unwilling to engage his former lord in single combat, Naotaka turned his back, allowing a squad of ''Ashigaru'' arquebusiers to open fire. Shigetada was struck by seven bullets and died standing, propped up by the remains of his spear.

===Shigetomo's Arrival and Breakdown===
Shigetomo's vanguard reached the smoking ruins of Toyozawa on the morning of June 8. Upon entering the charred remains of the Inner Sanctum, Shigetomo discovered the body of his father. The head had not been taken—an act of strange mercy by Naotaka—but the body had been riddled with bullets, a weapon Shigetada had championed his entire life.

Witnesses claim that Shigetomo did not weep. Instead, he fell into a catatonic state for several hours, sitting silently beside his father's corpse amidst the ash. When he finally stood, his demeanor had shifted from the rational, reformist bureaucrat to the erratic, vengeance-fueled "Punisher" of his youth.

He ordered the summary execution of the castle guards who had survived, blaming them for his father's death. He then refused to return to Okami to coordinate a proper response. Instead, he declared that he would "hunt the Red Devils to the ends of the earth," mobilizing the [[Hotta]] and [[Okudaira]] clans for an immediate, ill-advised pursuit that would lead directly to the tragedy at [[Ichijoudani]].

